---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';


const path = '/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-drip';
const title = 'Yotpo Email & SMS to Drip for Shopify: Migration Assessment';
const desc = 'Shopify order events and safe reconstruction of product-level email journeys after Yotpo messaging retirement.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I keep using Yotpo Email & SMS?",
    "answer": "No. The messaging product is retired; assess a replacement sender and keep Reviews and Loyalty as separate decisions."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should decide whether Drip fits?",
    "answer": "Shopify order events and safe reconstruction of product-level email journeys. Use the proposed acceptance cases in this guide before approving production enrollment."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does importing contacts restore every journey?",
    "answer": "No. Permissions, message history, dynamic segments and waiting automation contacts need separate reconciliation. Import success is not a sending approval."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Include Sequenzy if it is on your shortlist, using the same consent, Shopify event and operating-cost requirements. This page has not verified its current plan terms or migration tooling; it does not establish a price or feature advantage."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Yotpo to Drip</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="catalog-kicker">Migration assessment</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Yotpo Email &amp; SMS to Drip for Shopify</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Shopify order events and safe reconstruction of product-level email journeys. This guide proposes checks for a replacement program; it does not report merchant tests or measured outcomes.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>A retired sender changes the decision</h2>
      <p>Yotpo announced messaging retirement for the end of 2025. Its application now displays a decommission notice. Compare destinations and recovery options, rather than choosing to remain on that sender. <a class="link" href="https://email-and-sms.yotpo.com/">Yotpo lifecycle notice</a>.</p>
      <h2>What the Shopify connection supplies</h2>
      <p>Drip documents syncing Shopify customers, orders and products. It also explains that new profiles follow the Shopify email-marketing preference, and that syncing customers can increase billing. This is useful for reconstructing product-based audiences, but it does not establish a direct Yotpo migration service or recover Yotpo message engagement.</p>
      <h2>Choose an event deliberately</h2>
      <p>Drip distinguishes item-level events from order-level purchase events. Its purchase-event guide says parallel triggers behave as OR conditions and only the first triggering event is available in Liquid. Decide whether a journey needs one message per order or product-specific content. Recreating two old trigger names without checking their payloads can change what the email can render.</p>
      <h2>Proposed mixed-basket exercise</h2>
      <p>Use a hypothetical order containing a gift box and a refill pouch. The buyer should receive the intended order acknowledgment from its existing operational sender, while a proposed refill education journey must use the refill item rather than assume the gift recipient is the purchaser. Inspect the triggering payload and rendered product fields. Then cancel the refill and test the exclusion again. These are acceptance cases, not results from a store.</p>
      <h2>Keep subscription state outside guesswork</h2>
      <p>For replenishment, define the source of pause, cancellation and next-charge information if a subscription app is involved. A purchase event alone cannot tell the journey that a subscriber later paused. Give one person responsibility for mapping those changes and suspending reminders when the feed fails. Rebuild review-request ownership separately; Shopify order history does not prove a review was submitted or points were redeemed.</p>
      <h2>Decide whether workflow maintenance fits</h2>
      <p>Assess Drip when an operator can own product-event branches and inspect the resulting content. Before approval, ask that operator to change one product exclusion and explain which waiting contacts are affected. If nobody can maintain the event mapping after setup, include ongoing support in the decision. This practical exercise is more informative than a monthly revenue threshold or an unsupported canvas ranking.</p>
      <h2>History and active queues need different treatment</h2>
      <p>Keep Shopify order history, Yotpo message history and consent evidence as distinct records. For every still-active destination queue, record its sender, next action and stop condition. Waiting contacts are operational state, not simply an audience export. Reconstruct only the messages still justified by current purchase and permission data. If an import or event mapping fails, pause the replacement journey and reconcile it; the decommissioned service is not a rollback destination.</p>
      <h2>Price the work you will retain</h2>
      <p>Request current plan terms for the eligible audience, expected sends, channel usage and required integrations. Add retained Reviews/Loyalty charges and one-time reconstruction labor separately. Compare a normal month with your own peak calendar. No supplier price, revenue uplift or payback period is assumed here. A lower messaging quote can still leave more manual work if historical data or queue state cannot be recovered.</p>
      <h2>Evidence and remaining limits</h2>
      <p>Checked November 24, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424710485261-Shopify-Purchase-Events">Drip implementation documentation</a>. The acceptance designs above are editorial proposals. Account access, recovery availability, actual event payloads, legal sufficiency of permission records and live delivery remain untested. Obtain the missing evidence before enabling the affected journeys.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Migration guide</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Pricing models</a></p>
    </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-drip">Omnisend vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/yotpo-email-sms-vs-omnisend">Yotpo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/food-and-beverage">Email tools for food & beverage</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/subscription-boxes">Email tools for subscription boxes</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Yotpo to Drip FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
